# VRM/GLB fixtures are binary — never apply text/EOL conversion.
*.vrm binary
*.glb binary

# USDA fixtures, goldens, and baseline snapshots must check out with LF
# everywhere: a CRLF smudge changes the *content* of multi-line (triple-quoted)
# strings — the \r becomes part of the parsed string value — so a Windows
# checkout would flatten to different bytes than the committed golden
# (vrmschema-pr-windows L5 failure, PR #42).
*.usda text eol=lf

# Capture traces must check out with LF everywhere. The corpus test re-emits
# each committed trace through motionRuntime's writer -- which always writes
# '\n', so a shared fixture is byte-identical across the three OS cells -- and
# compares the raw bytes. A CRLF smudge on a Windows checkout would fail that
# comparison for every trace while the data itself was fine. Same reason as
# *.usda above.
*.trace text eol=lf

# Recorded VMC packet captures, for exactly the same reason: vrmAdapterVmc_corpus
# re-emits every committed capture through the adapter's writer and compares raw
# bytes. The writer always emits '\n', so a CRLF smudge on a Windows checkout
# would fail the comparison for every fixture while the recorded datagrams were
# fine.
*.vmcpackets text eol=lf

# Recorded mocopi packet captures, for the VMC reason plus one more: this corpus
# is also compared against the *generator* that authored it
# (vrmAdapterMocopi_packetGen), which writes '\n' explicitly, so a CRLF smudge
# would fail both the writer comparison and the generator comparison. The
# manifest's SHA-256 of each capture is a third thing a smudge would move.
*.mocopipackets text eol=lf

# BVH fixtures, for a third variant of the same reason: the corpus manifest
# records each file's size and SHA-256, and a CRLF smudge on a Windows checkout
# would change both. Hashes that differ per OS pin nothing. The parser accepts
# CRLF regardless -- motionBvh_parser proves it against text built in memory,
# where the bytes are the test's rather than the checkout's.
*.bvh text eol=lf
